<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Juby</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cabo Juby</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Juby is a headland on the Atlantic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, opposite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, beside the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a>. Under the Franco-Spanish agreements of 1912 the surrounding area became the southern zone of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in Morocco, administered from the post of Villa Bens. In the late 1920s it was a stop for French airmail flights, run for a time by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> handed it to Morocco in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-juby.jpg" alt="Cape Juby" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cradel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morocco_Protectorate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
